// @error cannot assign
// THE INLINE SPELLING OF A NESTED UNION, kept at a clean TYPE ERROR on purpose.
//
// `x: Shape | ParseError` written inline — as an annotation rather than as a declared
// `type Parsed = …` — registers through `registerInlineUnion`, which splits the SPELLING and
// sees `Shape` as one atom that `isStructAtom` rejects. Its nested arms (`Circle`, `Rect`) are
// therefore absent from the union's variant table, so there is no tag for a value built at one
// of them.
//
// THE SHAPE IS ALREADY BROKEN WITHOUT ANY LITERAL, which is what settles the column. Measured
// on master, with no object literal and no arm-pick involved:
//
//     const r: Rect = { kind: "rect", w: 3.0 }
//     const x: Shape | ParseError = r          // fails to compile
//
// So admitting the literal here — which the declared-alias sibling now does
// (`nested-union-arm-literal.vl`) — would move this spelling from a clean type error to a
// `wasm trap: cast failure`. That is a worse column for a shape that does not work either way,
// so the arm-pick's flatten is GATED on the outer union being a declared alias
// (`cUnionTyIxSet`), which is exactly the set `collectU` registers variants for.
//
// FLIPPING THIS means teaching `registerInlineUnion` the same flatten `collectU` gets for
// free by resolving member NAMES: expand an atom that names a union declaration into its own
// members before counting and pushing variants. Both halves move together, as they did for
// the declared form.
//
// The remedy today is one line — name the union:
//
//     type Parsed = Shape | ParseError
type Circle     = { kind: "circle", r: f64 }
type Rect       = { kind: "rect", w: f64 }
type ParseError = { why: string }
type Shape      = Circle | Rect

const x: Shape | ParseError = { kind: "rect", w: 3.0 }
if x is Rect { print(x.w) } else { print("no") }
